2026-09-14 05:30 slice 39: generic method monomorphization
- explicit-generic calls (M<T>(x), with or without a receiver) now
  specialize: the pre-pass clones the generic declaration, substitutes the
  type parameters through the cloned tree (types AND nested calls' sval2
  instantiation strings), registers the mangled copy (Name$Args) and
  rewrites the call site. The worklist re-scans appended specializations,
  so nested generic calls (Both<T> forwarding to Describe<T>/IdOf<T>)
  specialize transitively. Un-annotated calls to generic methods infer a
  single type parameter from the first argument's type. Generic
  declarations themselves are templates and are not emitted.
- supporting pieces: the parser's StripTypeArgs now records the explicit
  instantiation on the call's sval2 ("__"-joined) instead of dropping it,
  so ngen sees it after the binder; RunSpecPipeline builds a light locals
  table per scanned method so inference can type call arguments;
  KnownTyName filters still-open type parameters from registration;
  SubstTypeNode walks types and children (a..d included).
